#!/usr/bin/env bash
# heimdall-cleanup — the UNIFIED system-health CORE: one entrypoint for BOTH axes of
# machine health Heimdall is responsible for — MEMORY (RAM/swap pressure + hmd's OWN
# orphaned-python leak) and DISK (hmd's OWN reclaimable garbage: merged worktrees, temp,
# logs, retired claude versions). It ORCHESTRATES the two existing, tested reapers — it
# NEVER re-implements their logic:
#
#   • bin/heimdall-sysmon  — the MEMORY + hmd-orphan authority. Its `--filter-orphans`
#     matcher is the SINGLE source of truth for "is this a heimdall-owned python orphan?"
#     (ppid==1 AND a heimdall signature — a foreign python is NEVER matched). We route ALL
#     orphan scoping through it, and delegate the actual kill to its `--reap-hmd-orphans`
#     (which also stops the spawner so the leak can't respawn). Scope can never diverge.
#   • bin/heimdall-gc      — the DISK authority. Reaps ONLY hmd's own artifacts, each on a
#     proof of non-use (lsof-guarded retired versions, merged-only worktrees, TTL'd temp).
#     We drive it in --dry-run for `report` (mutates NOTHING) and full for `--apply`.
#   • bin/heimdall-deadcode — the CODE authority: which of hmd's OWN executables no live
#     entry point can reach. Garbage is not only processes and bytes on disk; a subsystem
#     nothing invokes is garbage too, and it is the kind that a green test suite hides.
#     bin/heimdall-brief sat built, tested and called by NOTHING for two months. This axis
#     is REPORT-ONLY in every mode — dead code is never auto-removed, because deciding
#     whether a tool is abandoned or merely standalone is a human's call, not a reaper's.
#
# WHY A NEW ENTRYPOINT (and not "promote sysmon"): sysmon is the memory/proc advisor; gc is
# the disk collector. Neither covers BOTH axes, and folding gc's disk domain into sysmon (or
# vice-versa) would DUPLICATE a tested reaper — the exact thing the conventions forbid. A
# thin orchestrator over the two authorities is the smallest possible "one core capability"
# surface: it adds unification + a safe auto path, and zero new reaping logic.
#
# THE READ-ONLY CONTRACT (the cc-selfheal lesson: a "status" that secretly mutated CAUSED the
# error it existed to prevent). `report` (the DEFAULT) and `--advise` are PROVABLY read-only:
# they call only `sysmon` (no mutating flag), `gc run --dry-run`, and read-only probes (df, ps,
# /proc). Neither can reach the reap path — dispatch routes report/advise and apply through
# disjoint functions.
#
# SUBCOMMANDS
#   heimdall-cleanup                 report (DEFAULT): read-only unified memory+disk health +
#                                    reclaimable-now total. Exit = worst severity (0/1/2).
#   heimdall-cleanup report          same, explicit.
#   heimdall-cleanup --json          machine-readable {severity,memory,disk,procs,reclaim}.
#   heimdall-cleanup --apply | reap  reap hmd's OWN garbage: orphaned hmd python (any present)
#                                    + gc disk. Prints reclaimed MB + freed resident RAM.
#   heimdall-cleanup --advise        READ-ONLY memory-pressure advisory for the SessionStart
#                                    hook: when swap/wired are genuinely HIGH (>= REBOOT_*_PCT)
#                                    it reports the two axes HONESTLY — swap is elastic (macOS
#                                    reclaims it unaided), wired RAM is kernel-side and a reboot
#                                    reclaims it only until its holder re-arms — and NAMES the
#                                    persistent holder when one is cheaply detectable. When the
#                                    pressure is NOT heimdall (no hmd python leak) it SAYS SO, so
#                                    the user is never left thinking hmd is the hog. Silent on a
#                                    healthy machine. Honors the opt-out. Emits ONE line (the
#                                    SessionStart output budget); add --verbose for the full
#                                    sixteen-line advisory. The DETECTION is identical either
#                                    way — verbosity changes rendering, never what is found.
#   heimdall-cleanup --code          READ-ONLY consumer audit: every executable in bin/ must
#                                    name what invokes it, or it is reported dead. Delegates
#                                    wholesale to bin/heimdall-deadcode. Deletes NOTHING.
#   heimdall-cleanup --deep          SUGGEST-only handoff to the mac-deep-clean skill — ONLY if
#                                    it's actually installed (never claims one that isn't) — for
#                                    the heavier, confirm-gated disk cleanup. Reaps NOTHING itself.
#   heimdall-cleanup --auto          the HOOK path (SessionStart/End). Runs gc (hmd disk) always;
#                                    reaps orphaned hmd python ONLY on a genuine runaway
#                                    (count >= ORPHAN_CRIT) so a live session's keeper is never
#                                    killed; SUGGESTS (one line) at/above ORPHAN_WARN. Honors the
#                                    opt-out. Non-spammy: silent unless it acted or there's pressure.
#   --quiet   suppress the human report (used by --auto).  --repo <path>  passthrough to gc.
#   --quick   passthrough to gc (temp+procs only; skips the slower worktree+version pass) —
#             used by the SessionStart hook so startup stays fast; SessionEnd runs the full sweep.
#
# OPT-OUT (mirrors heimdall-autoupdate): HEIMDALL_NO_CLEANUP=1 or ~/.heimdall/no-cleanup.
# Honored in the --auto/hook path so a normal run can fully disable the automatic cleanup.
#
# CROSS-PLATFORM HONESTY. sysmon's swap/wired grading is macOS-shaped (vm_stat / vm.swapusage).
# On Linux we do NOT stub it: memory pressure is read for real from /proc/meminfo, and the
# hmd-orphan scope still routes through sysmon's matcher (ppid==1 is orphaned-to-init there
# too). gc is portable (find/stat -c fallback). On an unknown OS memory grading is DECLARED
# unsupported (not faked) and only the disk/orphan axes run.
#
# EXIT: 0 healthy · 1 WARN (cleanup advisable) · 2 CRITICAL (act now / a live leak). --apply
# and --deep exit 0 on success.
set -uo pipefail

SELF_DIR="$(cd "$(dirname "${BASH_SOURCE[0]}")" && pwd)"
SYSMON="${HMD_CLEANUP_SYSMON:-$SELF_DIR/heimdall-sysmon}"
GC="${HMD_CLEANUP_GC:-$SELF_DIR/heimdall-gc}"
DEADCODE="${HMD_CLEANUP_DEADCODE:-$SELF_DIR/heimdall-deadcode}"

OS="${HMD_CLEANUP_OS:-$(uname -s 2>/dev/null || echo unknown)}"

# thresholds (shared with sysmon so the two never disagree on "is this a leak")
ORPHAN_WARN="${HMD_SYSMON_ORPHAN_WARN:-20}"    # >= → SUGGEST a reap
ORPHAN_CRIT="${HMD_SYSMON_ORPHAN_CRIT:-80}"    # >= → a runaway; --auto reaps unasked
SWAP_WARN_PCT="${HMD_SYSMON_SWAP_WARN_PCT:-40}"
MEM_AVAIL_WARN_PCT="${HMD_CLEANUP_MEM_AVAIL_WARN_PCT:-15}"   # linux: MemAvailable% below → warn
# memory-advisory thresholds: at/above these, the pressure is swap + kernel-wired RAM that
# heimdall CANNOT free. Set genuinely-high (above the WARN grades) so a healthy machine is
# never nagged. When crossed AND hmd is clean, the advisory says so honestly and describes
# what actually holds the memory instead of letting the user think hmd is the hog.
REBOOT_SWAP_PCT="${HMD_CLEANUP_REBOOT_SWAP_PCT:-80}"    # swap % of total in use → reboot-worthy
REBOOT_WIRED_PCT="${HMD_CLEANUP_REBOOT_WIRED_PCT:-80}"  # wired RAM % of total → reboot-worthy

# opt-out (env OR marker file), matching heimdall-autoupdate's idiom.
HMD_HOME="${HEIMDALL_HOME:-${HOME:-}/.heimdall}"
NO_CLEANUP_FILE="$HMD_HOME/no-cleanup"
opted_out() {
  [ "${HEIMDALL_NO_CLEANUP:-0}" = "1" ] && return 0
  [ -f "$NO_CLEANUP_FILE" ] && return 0
  return 1
}

# best-effort: is the mac-deep-clean skill actually installed? Checked at the same
# locations bin/detect-skills scans (user + project level) — NEVER claim a skill the
# user doesn't have (the launch-audit lesson: a suggestion is a claim, and pointing at
# an absent target makes it an overclaim).
_mac_deep_clean_installed() {
  [ -f "${HOME:-}/.claude/skills/mac-deep-clean/SKILL.md" ] && return 0
  [ -f "${REPO:-.}/.claude/skills/mac-deep-clean/SKILL.md" ] && return 0
  return 1
}

# ── test seams (never set in production) ─────────────────────────────────────────
#   HMD_CLEANUP_PS_ROWS   file of `pid ppid command…` rows, used instead of live ps for
#                         orphan scoping (lets a test plant a foreign proc + assert exclusion).
#   HMD_CLEANUP_KILL_CMD  a `cmd <pid>…`-recording stub used instead of sysmon --reap-hmd-orphans
#                         so a test proves the reap SCOPE without signalling any real process.
#   HMD_CLEANUP_OS        override for `uname -s`, so a test can pin the memory-probe path
#                         (Darwin→sysmon) deterministically on any CI host.
#   HMD_CLEANUP_PS_ROWS_FULL  file of `pid ppid pgid tty etime command…` rows, used instead of
#                         live ps for the UNATTRIBUTED bare-python reap scope (that oracle needs
#                         tty + age + pgid, which the plain row shape does not carry).
PS_ROWS_FILE="${HMD_CLEANUP_PS_ROWS:-}"
PS_ROWS_FULL_FILE="${HMD_CLEANUP_PS_ROWS_FULL:-}"
KILL_CMD="${HMD_CLEANUP_KILL_CMD:-}"

# how many unattributed bare-stdin python orphans it takes before --advise says anything.
# hmd cannot prove these are its own, so the floor is a genuine PILE-UP, not a handful.
BAREPY_ADVISE_MIN="${HMD_CLEANUP_BAREPY_ADVISE_MIN:-40}"

# ── argv ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
MODE="report"; JSON=0; QUIET=0; AUTO=0; QUICK=0; REPO=""; VERBOSE=0
while [ $# -gt 0 ]; do
  case "$1" in
    report)          MODE="report" ;;
    reap|--apply|apply) MODE="apply" ;;
    --advise|advise) MODE="advise" ;;
    --code|code)     MODE="code" ;;
    --deep|deep)     MODE="deep" ;;
    --auto)          MODE="apply"; AUTO=1; QUIET=1 ;;
    --quick)         QUICK=1 ;;
    --verbose|-v)    VERBOSE=1 ;;
    --json)          JSON=1 ;;
    --quiet|-q)      QUIET=1 ;;
    --repo)          REPO="${2:-}"; shift ;;
    --repo=*)        REPO="${1#--repo=}" ;;
    -h|--help)       grep '^#' "$0" | sed 's/^# \{0,1\}//'; exit 0 ;;
    *) echo "heimdall-cleanup: unknown argument: $1" >&2; exit 2 ;;
  esac
  shift
done

# ── shared probes (read-only) ────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# raw `pid ppid command…` rows — from the test seam, or live ps (both BSD + Linux forms).
_ps_rows_live() {
  if [ -n "$PS_ROWS_FILE" ]; then cat "$PS_ROWS_FILE" 2>/dev/null; return 0; fi
  ps -axo pid=,ppid=,command= 2>/dev/null || ps -eo pid=,ppid=,command= 2>/dev/null || true
}

# ONE ps snapshot per read-only run. Both consumers — the orphan scope and the wired-holder
# probe — read the SAME rows, so they can never disagree about what was running, and the
# advisory pays for ps once instead of twice (ps is the most expensive probe in the path).
# Priming must happen OUTSIDE a command substitution or the cache is lost with the subshell,
# so do_report/do_advise call _ps_prime at their top level. Unprimed callers (do_apply) fall
# through to a live read exactly as before.
_PS_ROWS_CACHE=""; _PS_ROWS_PRIMED=0
_ps_prime() {
  [ "$_PS_ROWS_PRIMED" = 1 ] && return 0
  _PS_ROWS_CACHE="$(_ps_rows_live)"
  _PS_ROWS_PRIMED=1
  return 0
}
_ps_rows() {
  if [ "$_PS_ROWS_PRIMED" = 1 ]; then printf '%s\n' "$_PS_ROWS_CACHE"; return 0; fi
  _ps_rows_live
}

# the reap SCOPE — delegated entirely to sysmon's matcher (single source of truth).
_orphan_pids() { _ps_rows | "$SYSMON" --filter-orphans 2>/dev/null || true; }

# ── the UNATTRIBUTED bare-stdin python orphans (`python3 -` at ppid 1) ───────────────────
# These are the pile hmd used to be blind to: no script path, no marker, nothing in the command
# line that proves whose they are. Scoping again routes through sysmon so detection and reaping
# cannot diverge, and NOTHING here claims them as heimdall's.

# rich `pid ppid pgid tty etime command…` rows — the reap oracle needs tty + age + pgid.
_ps_rows_full() {
  if [ -n "$PS_ROWS_FULL_FILE" ]; then cat "$PS_ROWS_FULL_FILE" 2>/dev/null; return 0; fi
  ps -axo pid=,ppid=,pgid=,tty=,etime=,command= 2>/dev/null \
    || ps -eo pid=,ppid=,pgid=,tty=,etime=,command= 2>/dev/null || true
}

# This reaper's own exclusion set — every ancestor pid up the tree, plus our pgid and sid, so
# the session that INVOKED us can never be inside its own reap set. Identical in shape to
# bin/heimdall-reap-idle's _self_exclude (the convention this repo already reaps by), and it
# MERGES any operator-supplied HMD_BAREPY_SELF_EXCLUDE rather than replacing it.
_barepy_self_exclude() {
  local p="$$" acc="" guard=0 pgid sid
  while [ -n "$p" ] && [ "$p" != "0" ] && [ "$guard" -lt 64 ]; do
    acc="$acc $p"
    p="$(ps -o ppid= -p "$p" 2>/dev/null | tr -d ' ')"
    guard=$((guard+1))
  done
  pgid="$( (ps -o pgid= -p $$ 2>/dev/null || echo '') | tr -d ' ' )"
  sid="$( (ps -o sess= -p $$ 2>/dev/null || echo '') | tr -d ' ' )"
  printf '%s %s %s %s' "$acc" "$pgid" "$sid" "${HMD_BAREPY_SELF_EXCLUDE:-}"
}

# the guarded REAP set: aged past the floor, no controlling tty, outside our own tree.
_barepy_reapable_pids() {
  _ps_rows_full | HMD_BAREPY_SELF_EXCLUDE="$(_barepy_self_exclude)" \
    "$SYSMON" --filter-reapable-python-orphans 2>/dev/null || true
}

# the DETECTED count. Prefers the figure sysmon already returned in the --json read that
# _probe_memory performed (so the SessionStart path pays no second ps), and falls back to the
# oracle when that read carried no such field — an older sysmon or a non-Darwin host must
# degrade to a slower count, never to a silent zero. Reporting 0 because the cheap path was
# unavailable would rebuild the exact blindness this field exists to remove.
barepy_n=0; barepy_from_json=0   # filled by _probe_memory when sysmon's --json carries the field
_barepy_count() {
  if [ "$barepy_from_json" = 1 ]; then printf '%s\n' "$barepy_n"; return 0; fi
  _ps_rows | "$SYSMON" --filter-bare-python-orphans 2>/dev/null | grep -c . || echo 0
}

# resident RAM (MB) held by a set of pids — the REAL memory a reap frees back (wired/swap
# need a reboot; we never claim otherwise). 0 for synthetic test pids that don't exist.
_rss_mb() {
  [ "$#" -gt 0 ] || { echo 0; return 0; }
  local list kb
  list="$(printf '%s,' "$@")"; list="${list%,}"
  kb="$(ps -o rss= -p "$list" 2>/dev/null | awk '{s+=$1} END{printf "%d", s+0}')"
  echo $(( (kb + 512) / 1024 ))
}

# gc receipt (stdout) for disk accounting. dry -> --dry-run (mutates nothing).
_gc_run() {
  local extra=""
  [ -n "$REPO" ] && extra="--repo $REPO"
  [ "$QUICK" = 1 ] && extra="$extra --quick"
  if [ "$1" = "dry" ]; then
    "$GC" run --dry-run $extra 2>/dev/null || true
  else
    "$GC" run $extra 2>/dev/null || true
  fi
}
_gc_kb() { sed -n 's/.*temp file(s) ~\([0-9]*\)KB.*/\1/p' | head -1; }
_gc_mb() { sed -n 's/.*claude version(s) ~\([0-9]*\)MB.*/\1/p' | head -1; }
_gc_wt() { sed -n 's/.*freed \([0-9]*\) worktree(s).*/\1/p' | head -1; }
_gc_tempn() { sed -n 's/.*, \([0-9]*\) temp file(s).*/\1/p' | head -1; }
_gc_ccn() { sed -n 's/.*, \([0-9]*\) retired claude version(s).*/\1/p' | head -1; }
_num() { case "$1" in ''|*[!0-9]*) echo 0 ;; *) echo "$1" ;; esac; }

# ── memory pressure: macOS via sysmon, Linux via /proc/meminfo (REAL, not stubbed) ──
mem_status="ok"; mem_desc="unsupported on $OS"; wired_pct=0; swap_pct=0; avail_pct=0
swap_used_g=0; swap_total_g=0
_probe_memory() {
  if [ "$OS" = "Darwin" ] && [ -x "$SYSMON" ]; then
    local js
    js="$("$SYSMON" --json 2>/dev/null || true)"
    mem_status="$(printf '%s' "$js" | sed -n 's/.*"memory":{"status":"\([a-z]*\)".*/\1/p')"
    swap_pct="$(_num "$(printf '%s' "$js" | sed -n 's/.*"swap_used_pct":\([0-9]*\).*/\1/p')")"
    wired_pct="$(_num "$(printf '%s' "$js" | sed -n 's/.*"wired_pct":\([0-9]*\).*/\1/p')")"
    swap_used_g="$(printf '%s' "$js" | sed -n 's/.*"swap_used_g":\([0-9.]*\).*/\1/p')"; [ -n "$swap_used_g" ] || swap_used_g=0
    swap_total_g="$(printf '%s' "$js" | sed -n 's/.*"swap_total_g":\([0-9.]*\).*/\1/p')"; [ -n "$swap_total_g" ] || swap_total_g=0
    # the UNATTRIBUTED bare-stdin python orphan count, taken from the read we already paid
    # for. barepy_from_json records whether the field was actually PRESENT: an older sysmon
    # emits no such key, and treating that silence as 0 would rebuild the exact blindness
    # this field exists to remove. _barepy_count falls back to the oracle when it is unset.
    local bp
    bp="$(printf '%s' "$js" | sed -n 's/.*"python_orphans_unattributed":\([0-9]*\).*/\1/p')"
    if [ -n "$bp" ]; then barepy_n="$bp"; barepy_from_json=1; fi
    [ -n "$mem_status" ] || mem_status="ok"
    mem_desc="swap ${swap_used_g}G/${swap_total_g}G (${swap_pct}%) · wired ${wired_pct}%"
    return 0
  fi
  if [ -r /proc/meminfo ]; then
    local total avail stot sfree
    total="$(awk '/^MemTotal:/{print $2}' /proc/meminfo 2>/dev/null)"
    avail="$(awk '/^MemAvailable:/{print $2}' /proc/meminfo 2>/dev/null)"
    stot="$(awk '/^SwapTotal:/{print $2}' /proc/meminfo 2>/dev/null)"
    sfree="$(awk '/^SwapFree:/{print $2}' /proc/meminfo 2>/dev/null)"
    total="$(_num "$total")"; avail="$(_num "$avail")"; stot="$(_num "$stot")"; sfree="$(_num "$sfree")"
    if [ "$total" -gt 0 ]; then avail_pct=$(( avail * 100 / total )); fi
    if [ "$stot" -gt 0 ]; then swap_pct=$(( (stot - sfree) * 100 / stot )); fi
    mem_desc="avail ${avail_pct}% · swap ${swap_pct}%"
    mem_status="ok"
    [ "$swap_pct" -ge "$SWAP_WARN_PCT" ] && mem_status="warn"
    [ "$avail_pct" -le "$MEM_AVAIL_WARN_PCT" ] && [ "$total" -gt 0 ] && mem_status="warn"
    return 0
  fi
  # unknown OS: declare unsupported honestly; never fabricate a memory grade.
  mem_status="ok"; mem_desc="memory grading unsupported on $OS (no vm_stat, no /proc/meminfo)"
}

# ── disk pressure: reuse sysmon's whole-volume read on macOS; df elsewhere ─────────
disk_status="ok"; disk_desc=""
_probe_disk() {
  if [ "$OS" = "Darwin" ] && [ -x "$SYSMON" ]; then
    local js used free
    js="$("$SYSMON" --json 2>/dev/null || true)"
    disk_status="$(printf '%s' "$js" | sed -n 's/.*"disk":{"status":"\([a-z]*\)".*/\1/p')"
    used="$(_num "$(printf '%s' "$js" | sed -n 's/.*"used_pct":\([0-9]*\).*/\1/p')")"
    [ -n "$disk_status" ] || disk_status="ok"
    disk_desc="${used}% used (Data volume)"
    return 0
  fi
  local line used
  line="$(df -Pk / 2>/dev/null | awk 'NR==2{print $5}' | tr -d '%')"
  used="$(_num "$line")"
  disk_desc="${used}% used (/)"
  disk_status="ok"
  [ "$used" -ge 85 ] && disk_status="warn"
  [ "$used" -ge 93 ] && disk_status="crit"
}

_rank() { case "$1" in crit) echo 2 ;; warn) echo 1 ;; *) echo 0 ;; esac; }

# ── memory advisory (HONEST attribution) ──────────────────────────────────────────
# genuinely-HIGH memory pressure = swap OR wired at/above the advisory thresholds. This is
# swap + kernel-wired RAM that heimdall did not cause and cannot free. Reads the real
# vm_stat/vm.swapusage figures (via sysmon) that _probe_memory already set.
_reboot_worthy() {
  [ "$swap_pct" -ge "$REBOOT_SWAP_PCT" ] 2>/dev/null && return 0
  [ "$wired_pct" -ge "$REBOOT_WIRED_PCT" ] 2>/dev/null && return 0
  return 1
}

# ── the ORPHAN axis of the advisory gate (INDEPENDENT of memory) ──────────────────
# An orphan pile and memory pressure are separate failures with separate causes. The
# advisory used to be reachable only through _reboot_worthy, which made the orphan monitor
# a passenger on the memory monitor: on a machine with healthy RAM the pile could grow
# without bound and nothing was said. That is the same defect the whole orphan-detection
# effort exists to kill — a monitor whose silence is indistinguishable from health — one
# level up from where it was first found.
#
# Both floors already exist and are REUSED rather than re-invented, so the advisory can
# never disagree with the report about what counts as a leak:
#   · ORPHAN_WARN       — the count at which `report` already suggests a reap (hmd's OWN).
#   · BAREPY_ADVISE_MIN — the pile-up floor for orphans hmd cannot attribute. Deliberately
#     higher: pointing an operator at a reaper over a handful of processes hmd cannot even
#     prove are its own is how a real signal gets tuned out before it matters.
#   $1 attributable hmd orphans   $2 unattributed bare-stdin orphans
_orphan_worthy() {
  [ "$(_num "${1:-0}")" -ge "$ORPHAN_WARN" ] 2>/dev/null && return 0
  [ "$(_num "${2:-0}")" -ge "$BAREPY_ADVISE_MIN" ] 2>/dev/null && return 0
  return 1
}

# the ADVISORY GATE: the UNION of the two axes. Either one alone is enough to speak; both
# together still produce ONE line (the SessionStart output budget), never two.
_advisory_worthy() { _reboot_worthy || _orphan_worthy "$@"; }

# ── persistent wired-memory holders (CHEAP detection; PRINT-PATH ONLY) ────────────
# Wired RAM is kernel-side: heimdall cannot free it, and neither does a reboot DURABLY — a
# restart drops the pages, but any holder that re-arms at boot re-wires them straight back.
# So the only durable advice is to name the HOLDER, and we name one ONLY when we can see it.
# Two probes, both cheap, neither walking the filesystem:
#   · mount(8)  — one getmntinfo read. Mounted OS/simulator runtime volumes hold wired kernel
#                 memory continuously and re-mount at every boot (the case measured here).
#   · ps rows   — already collected for the orphan scope (see _ps_prime), so this costs
#                 nothing extra. A running hypervisor wires its guest RAM for the VM's life.
# Detecting a holder is NOT a claim that it accounts for all wired RAM — it is a candidate
# the user can act on. When neither probe sees anything we print NOTHING and the caller falls
# back to saying the holder is unidentified: an advisory that confidently prescribes the wrong
# action is worse than one that describes the situation and stops. Both probes fail quiet, so
# a broken detector degrades the wording instead of breaking SessionStart.
MOUNT_CMD="${HMD_CLEANUP_MOUNT_CMD:-/sbin/mount}"

_wired_holders() {
  local simvols vms
  simvols="$(_num "$("$MOUNT_CMD" 2>/dev/null | grep -c 'CoreSimulator' 2>/dev/null || true)")"
  if [ "$simvols" -gt 0 ]; then
    printf '      · %s mounted simulator runtime volume(s) — a reboot re-mounts and re-wires them\n' "$simvols"
    printf '        review:  xcrun simctl runtime list\n'
    printf '        durable: sudo xcrun simctl runtime delete <build-id>   (UNUSED runtimes only)\n'
  fi
  vms="$(_ps_rows 2>/dev/null \
    | grep -Eo 'com\.docker\.(virtualization|hyperkit)|vmware-vmx|prl_vm_app|VBoxHeadless|VirtualBoxVM|qemu-system-[a-z0-9_]+|Virtualization\.VirtualMachine' 2>/dev/null \
    | sort -u | tr '\n' ' ')"
  vms="${vms% }"
  if [ -n "$vms" ]; then
    printf '      · running hypervisor/VM: %s — guest RAM stays wired for the VM'\''s lifetime\n' "$vms"
    printf '        durable: shut the VM down, or lower its RAM allocation\n'
  fi
  return 0
}

# the DISK axis, reported SEPARATELY. Reclaiming hmd disk does not relieve memory pressure,
# and a --quick scan deliberately skips the worktree + retired-version passes — so its total
# is PARTIAL. We say that instead of quoting a partial figure as the hmd-owned total.
#   $1 hmd-reclaimable disk MB (meaningful only on a full scan)
_disk_axis_note() {
  if [ "$QUICK" = 1 ]; then
    printf '    disk (separate axis — reclaiming it does not relieve memory): this quick scan\n'
    printf '          skips the worktree + retired-version passes, so hmd'\''s disk figure is NOT\n'
    printf '          measured here. Run `heimdall-cleanup report` for the full number.\n'
  else
    printf '    disk (separate axis — reclaiming it does not relieve memory): ~%sMB of hmd-owned\n' "$1"
    printf '          garbage is reclaimable now.\n'
  fi
  return 0
}

# print the memory advisory. Attribution is the whole point:
#   · hmd's OWN share (orphaned python) is named and reaped FIRST when there is one, and the
#     figure is labelled MEMORY axis so it is never read as covering disk too;
#   · swap is reported as ELASTIC — macOS sizes it on demand and reclaims it on its own as
#     pressure eases, so a high swap % is a symptom to watch, not by itself an action;
#   · wired RAM is reported as kernel-side and REBOOT-TEMPORARY, with the persistent holder
#     named when one is detectable and declared unidentified when it is not.
# Every number is a real reading.
#   $1 orphans   $2 resident-MB held by those orphans   $3 hmd-reclaimable disk MB
#   $4 unattributed bare-stdin orphans (optional; only the ORPHAN-axis branch renders it)
_reboot_advisory() {
  local orphans="$1" rss="$2" reclaim_mb="$3" barepy holders
  barepy="$(_num "${4:-0}")"

  # THE ORPHAN AXIS ON ITS OWN. Reached when the pile crossed its floor and memory did NOT:
  # saying "memory pressure is HIGH" here would fabricate a reading the probe never took, so
  # this branch states what was actually measured and explains why it is speaking anyway.
  # The wired-RAM/holder paragraphs below are deliberately absent — there is no wired-RAM
  # problem to explain, and padding an advisory with irrelevant remedies is how it stops
  # being read.
  if ! _reboot_worthy; then
    printf '  • memory is HEALTHY (swap %sG/%sG = %s%%, wired %s%%) — this advisory is on the ORPHAN\n' \
      "$swap_used_g" "$swap_total_g" "$swap_pct" "$wired_pct"
    printf '    axis, which moves independently of memory. A pile accumulates whether or not RAM is\n'
    printf '    tight, so a quiet memory reading is NOT evidence of a clean process table.\n'
    if [ "$orphans" -gt 0 ]; then
      printf '    %s orphaned hmd python at ppid 1 holding ~%sMB resident (MEMORY axis). This one IS\n' "$orphans" "$rss"
      printf '    heimdall'\''s, and heimdall says so. Reap its OWN leak:\n'
      printf '        heimdall-cleanup --apply\n'
    else
      printf '    no ATTRIBUTABLE heimdall python leak — 0 hmd-owned orphans.\n'
    fi
    if [ "$barepy" -gt 0 ]; then
      printf '    %s unattributed bare-stdin python orphan(s) at ppid 1. hmd CANNOT prove these are\n' "$barepy"
      printf '    its own — a bare python3 - command line carries no attribution — so it neither\n'
      printf '    claims nor denies them, and NEVER reaps them unasked. Inspect them first:\n'
      printf '        ps -Ao pid,ppid,lstart,tty,command | awk %s\n' "'\$2==1 && /python3 -\$/'"
      if [ "$barepy" -ge "$BAREPY_ADVISE_MIN" ] 2>/dev/null; then
        printf '    then reap ONLY the aged, detached, outside-your-own-tree ones:\n'
        printf '        heimdall-cleanup --apply\n'
      fi
    fi
    _disk_axis_note "$reclaim_mb"
    return 0
  fi

  holders="$(_wired_holders 2>/dev/null || true)"

  if [ "$orphans" -gt 0 ]; then
    printf '  • memory pressure is HIGH (swap %sG/%sG = %s%%, wired %s%%). Part of it IS heimdall —\n' \
      "$swap_used_g" "$swap_total_g" "$swap_pct" "$wired_pct"
    printf '    %s orphaned hmd python holding ~%sMB resident (MEMORY axis). Reap heimdall'\''s OWN\n' "$orphans" "$rss"
    printf '    leak FIRST:\n'
    printf '        heimdall-cleanup --apply\n'
  else
    printf '  • memory pressure is HIGH (swap %sG/%sG = %s%%, wired %s%%) — and this is NOT heimdall:\n' \
      "$swap_used_g" "$swap_total_g" "$swap_pct" "$wired_pct"
    printf '    no hmd python leak — 0 heimdall processes, ~%sMB of this RAM is heimdall'\''s (MEMORY axis).\n' "$rss"
  fi

  printf '    swap: macOS sizes swap on demand and reclaims it on its own as pressure eases,\n'
  printf '          without a reboot. A high swap %% is a symptom to watch, not by itself an action.\n'
  printf '    wired: kernel-side. Heimdall cannot free kernel-wired memory — and neither does a\n'
  printf '           reboot, durably: a restart drops the pages, but a holder that re-arms at\n'
  printf '           boot re-wires them. A reboot is a TEMPORARY reclaim, not the fix; removing\n'
  printf '           the holder is.\n'
  if [ -n "$holders" ]; then
    printf '    persistent holders visible from here:\n'
    printf '%s\n' "$holders"
    printf '      → remove the holder for a durable reclaim; a reboot only buys time\n'
  else
    printf '    no persistent holder identified from here — wired RAM is held by kernel-side\n'
    printf '    consumers heimdall cannot enumerate (VMs, OS/simulator runtimes, drivers, system\n'
    printf '    extensions). If wired RAM returns after a restart, the holder is persistent.\n'
    printf '      → identify the persistent holder before treating a reboot as the fix\n'
  fi
  _disk_axis_note "$reclaim_mb"
  return 0
}

# the ONE-LINE form of the same advisory, for the SessionStart hook.
#
# The full block above is sixteen lines and the machine paid for all of them at the top of
# every session, to describe a condition that typically had not changed since the last one.
# A hook gets one line unless it is actionable — so this keeps ONLY the parts that change a
# decision and routes the explanation to `--advise --verbose`:
#   · the MEASURED figures, so the claim is never bare;
#   · the ATTRIBUTION, which is the whole reason this advisory exists — heimdall says out
#     loud when the pressure is NOT its fault, and equally when part of it IS;
#   · hmd's OWN remedy (`--apply`) whenever there is one to run, because that is the single
#     case the machine can actually act on;
#   · that a reboot is a TEMPORARY reclaim, so the terse form can never be read as endorsing
#     the "restart is the fix" claim the full block exists to retract.
# What it drops is explanation, not detection: every probe above still runs.
_reboot_advisory_line() {
  local orphans="$1" rss="$2" barepy holders held=""
  barepy="$(_num "${3:-0}")"
  holders="$(_wired_holders 2>/dev/null || true)"
  [ -n "$holders" ] && held=" a persistent holder IS visible from here —"
  # THE 2026-08-08 RETRACTION. On that day this line said "NOT heimdall" while 224 orphaned
  # `python3 -` processes sat at ppid 1 — hmd's own heredocs, invisible to a matcher that
  # requires a marker the shape cannot carry. The flat denial is now reserved for a process
  # table with NOTHING unexplained in it. When unattributed orphans are present hmd says the
  # honest thing instead: it has no ATTRIBUTABLE leak, and there is a pile it cannot claim
  # either way. It never asserts ownership it cannot prove, and never denies one it cannot
  # rule out. The remedy is offered only past the pile-up floor, because pointing an operator
  # at a reaper over a handful of processes is how a real signal gets tuned out.
  if [ "$orphans" -le 0 ] && [ "$barepy" -gt 0 ] 2>/dev/null; then
    local remedy=" hmd will not reap them blindly."
    [ "$barepy" -ge "$BAREPY_ADVISE_MIN" ] 2>/dev/null && \
      remedy=" Reap only the aged+detached ones: heimdall-cleanup --apply ·"
    printf '[heimdall] memory pressure HIGH (swap %sG/%sG = %s%%, wired %s%%) — no ATTRIBUTABLE hmd leak (~%sMB MEMORY axis), but %s unattributed bare-stdin python orphan(s) at ppid 1: ownership UNPROVEN, so hmd neither claims nor denies them.%s%s detail: heimdall-cleanup --advise --verbose\n' \
      "$swap_used_g" "$swap_total_g" "$swap_pct" "$wired_pct" "$rss" "$barepy" "$remedy" "$held"
    return 0
  fi
  if [ "$orphans" -gt 0 ]; then
    # An attributable leak does not make the unattributed pile disappear; reaping hmd's own
    # orphans and finding the pressure unchanged is exactly how an operator concludes hmd
    # lied to them. So the second number rides along, still labelled unproven.
    local also=""
    [ "$barepy" -gt 0 ] 2>/dev/null && also=" + ${barepy} unattributed bare-stdin orphan(s), ownership unproven ·"
    printf '[heimdall] memory pressure HIGH (swap %sG/%sG = %s%%, wired %s%%) — Part of it IS heimdall: %s orphaned hmd python holding ~%sMB (MEMORY axis).%s Reap hmd'\''s own leak FIRST: heimdall-cleanup --apply · a reboot is only a temporary reclaim ·%s detail: heimdall-cleanup --advise --verbose\n' \
      "$swap_used_g" "$swap_total_g" "$swap_pct" "$wired_pct" "$orphans" "$rss" "$also" "$held"
  else
    printf '[heimdall] memory pressure HIGH (swap %sG/%sG = %s%%, wired %s%%) — NOT heimdall (no hmd python leak, ~%sMB MEMORY axis). Wired RAM is kernel-side: a reboot is only a temporary reclaim, removing the holder is the fix ·%s detail: heimdall-cleanup --advise --verbose\n' \
      "$swap_used_g" "$swap_total_g" "$swap_pct" "$wired_pct" "$rss" "$held"
  fi
  return 0
}

# the ONE-LINE form for the ORPHAN axis: memory is FINE and the pile is the entire reason
# anything is being said. Same attribution discipline as the memory-pressure line above —
# hmd claims what it can prove and names what it cannot — with one difference: no memory
# prescription rides along, because there is no memory problem to prescribe for. Inventing
# a "pressure is HIGH" preamble to reuse the existing sentence would put a figure in front
# of the operator that no probe ever measured.
#   $1 orphans   $2 resident-MB held by those orphans   $3 unattributed bare-stdin orphans
_orphan_advisory_line() {
  local orphans="$1" rss="$2" barepy own="" unattr="" remedy=""
  barepy="$(_num "${3:-0}")"
  if [ "$orphans" -gt 0 ] 2>/dev/null; then
    own=" ${orphans} orphaned hmd python holding ~${rss}MB (MEMORY axis) is heimdall's OWN leak."
    remedy=" Reap hmd's own leak: heimdall-cleanup --apply ·"
  fi
  if [ "$barepy" -gt 0 ] 2>/dev/null; then
    unattr=" ${barepy} unattributed bare-stdin python orphan(s): ownership UNPROVEN, hmd neither claims nor denies them."
    # the remedy for the unproven pile is offered ONLY past the pile-up floor, and only ever
    # as the GUARDED subset (aged + detached + outside your own tree). hmd must never read as
    # volunteering to kill processes it cannot prove are its own.
    if [ "$orphans" -le 0 ] 2>/dev/null; then
      if [ "$barepy" -ge "$BAREPY_ADVISE_MIN" ] 2>/dev/null; then
        remedy=" Reap only the aged+detached ones: heimdall-cleanup --apply ·"
      else
        unattr="$unattr hmd will not reap them blindly."
      fi
    fi
  fi
  printf '[heimdall] orphan pile at ppid 1 — memory is HEALTHY (swap %sG/%sG = %s%%, wired %s%%), these are INDEPENDENT axes:%s%s%s detail: heimdall-cleanup --advise --verbose\n' \
    "$swap_used_g" "$swap_total_g" "$swap_pct" "$wired_pct" "$own" "$unattr" "$remedy"
  return 0
}

# ── REPORT (read-only) ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
do_report() {
  _ps_prime          # one ps snapshot shared by the orphan scope and the wired-holder probe
  _probe_memory
  _probe_disk
  local pids orphans rss
  pids="$(_orphan_pids)"
  orphans="$(printf '%s\n' "$pids" | grep -c . || true)"; orphans="$(_num "$orphans")"
  # shellcheck disable=SC2086
  rss="$([ "$orphans" -gt 0 ] && _rss_mb $pids || echo 0)"

  local barepy
  barepy="$(_num "$(_barepy_count)")"

  local proc_status="ok"
  [ "$orphans" -ge "$ORPHAN_WARN" ] && proc_status="warn"
  [ "$orphans" -ge "$ORPHAN_CRIT" ] && proc_status="crit"

  local receipt kb mb wt tempn ccn reclaim_mb
  receipt="$(_gc_run dry)"
  kb="$(_num "$(printf '%s' "$receipt" | _gc_kb)")"
  mb="$(_num "$(printf '%s' "$receipt" | _gc_mb)")"
  wt="$(_num "$(printf '%s' "$receipt" | _gc_wt)")"
  tempn="$(_num "$(printf '%s' "$receipt" | _gc_tempn)")"
  ccn="$(_num "$(printf '%s' "$receipt" | _gc_ccn)")"
  reclaim_mb=$(( (kb + 1023) / 1024 + mb ))

  local sev=0 r
  for s in "$mem_status" "$disk_status" "$proc_status"; do r="$(_rank "$s")"; [ "$r" -gt "$sev" ] && sev="$r"; done
  local sev_word="ok"; [ "$sev" = 1 ] && sev_word="warn"; [ "$sev" = 2 ] && sev_word="crit"

  if [ "$JSON" = 1 ]; then
    printf '{'
    printf '"severity":"%s",' "$sev_word"
    printf '"memory":{"status":"%s","swap_pct":%s,"wired_pct":%s,"avail_pct":%s},' \
      "$mem_status" "$swap_pct" "$wired_pct" "$avail_pct"
    printf '"disk":{"status":"%s"},' "$disk_status"
    printf '"procs":{"status":"%s","hmd_orphans":%s,"resident_mb":%s,"python_orphans_unattributed":%s},' \
      "$proc_status" "$orphans" "$rss" "$barepy"
    printf '"reclaim":{"disk_mb":%s,"temp_files":%s,"temp_kb":%s,"cc_versions":%s,"cc_mb":%s,"worktrees":%s}' \
      "$reclaim_mb" "$tempn" "$kb" "$ccn" "$mb" "$wt"
    printf '}\n'
    return "$sev"
  fi

  if [ "$QUIET" = 1 ] && [ "$sev" = 0 ] && [ "$reclaim_mb" = 0 ] && [ "$orphans" = 0 ]; then
    return 0
  fi

  local _l="  %-8s %-5s %s\n"
  printf 'heimdall-cleanup — system health: %s\n' "$(printf '%s' "$sev_word" | tr '[:lower:]' '[:upper:]')"
  printf "$_l" "memory" "$mem_status" "$mem_desc"
  # "no heimdall python leak" is a claim about what hmd can ATTRIBUTE, and it stays a separate
  # sentence from the pile it cannot. Reporting only the first number is what made 224 live
  # processes read as a clean machine on 2026-08-08.
  local unattr=""
  [ "$barepy" -gt 0 ] && unattr=" · ${barepy} unattributed bare-stdin python orphan(s) at ppid 1 (ownership unproven)"
  if [ "$orphans" -gt 0 ]; then
    printf "$_l" "procs" "$proc_status" "${orphans} orphaned heimdall python (~${rss}MB resident, reap-safe)${unattr}"
  else
    printf "$_l" "procs" "$proc_status" "no ATTRIBUTABLE heimdall python leak${unattr}"
  fi
  printf "$_l" "disk" "$disk_status" "$disk_desc"
  printf "$_l" "reclaim" "-" "hmd-owned: ${tempn} temp ~${kb}KB · ${ccn} retired claude version(s) ~${mb}MB · ${wt} merged worktree(s) → ~${reclaim_mb}MB reclaimable now"

  if [ "$sev" = 0 ] && [ "$reclaim_mb" = 0 ] && [ "$orphans" = 0 ]; then
    printf 'suggestion: none — healthy.\n'
    return 0
  fi
  printf 'suggestion:\n'
  if [ "$orphans" -ge "$ORPHAN_WARN" ] || [ "$reclaim_mb" -gt 0 ]; then
    printf '  • reap heimdall'\''s OWN garbage (foreign procs + user files untouched):\n'
    printf '      heimdall-cleanup --apply\n'
  fi
  if _reboot_worthy; then
    _reboot_advisory "$orphans" "$rss" "$reclaim_mb" "$barepy"
  elif [ "$mem_status" != "ok" ]; then
    printf '  • memory pressure: reaping hmd'\''s own footprint does not touch wired RAM. macOS\n'
    printf '    reclaims swap on its own as pressure eases; wired RAM needs its holder removed.\n'
  fi
  if [ "$disk_status" != "ok" ]; then
    printf '  • disk is under pressure — heavier cleanup (caches / dead repos / SDK bloat):\n'
    printf '      heimdall-cleanup --deep\n'
  fi
  return "$sev"
}

# ── APPLY (reap hmd's OWN garbage only) ───────────────────────────────────────────
do_apply() {
  if [ "$AUTO" = 1 ] && opted_out; then return 0; fi

  local pids orphans rss threshold reaped=0
  pids="$(_orphan_pids)"
  orphans="$(printf '%s\n' "$pids" | grep -c . || true)"; orphans="$(_num "$orphans")"
  # manual --apply reaps ANY present hmd orphan; --auto reaps ONLY a genuine runaway
  # (>= ORPHAN_CRIT) so a normal session's live presence keeper is never killed.
  threshold=1; [ "$AUTO" = 1 ] && threshold="$ORPHAN_CRIT"

  # shellcheck disable=SC2086
  rss="$([ "$orphans" -gt 0 ] && _rss_mb $pids || echo 0)"

  if [ "$orphans" -ge "$threshold" ] && [ "$orphans" -gt 0 ]; then
    if [ -n "$KILL_CMD" ]; then
      # controlled/test path: signal EXACTLY the scoped pids, nothing else.
      # shellcheck disable=SC2086
      $KILL_CMD $pids 2>/dev/null || true
      reaped="$orphans"
    else
      # production: delegate to sysmon's reaper (re-derives the same scope + stops the spawner).
      "$SYSMON" --reap-hmd-orphans >/dev/null 2>&1 || true
      reaped="$orphans"
    fi
  fi

  # ── the UNATTRIBUTED pile: MANUAL --apply only, and only the guarded subset ─────────────
  # These are not provably hmd's, so the rules are deliberately stricter than above:
  #   · --auto NEVER touches them. An unattended hook killing a process on a guess is worse
  #     than the blindness it replaced — a wrong reap destroys someone's work silently.
  #   · the scope comes from sysmon's reap oracle, which requires aged + detached + outside
  #     this caller's own tree, so it is always a strict subset of what is merely detected.
  #   · signalling is per-pid: a batched `kill pid pid pid…` was measured on 2026-08-08 to
  #     leave the whole set alive when any one pid had already exited.
  local bp_pids bp_n=0
  if [ "$AUTO" != 1 ]; then
    bp_pids="$(_barepy_reapable_pids)"
    bp_n="$(printf '%s\n' "$bp_pids" | grep -c . || true)"; bp_n="$(_num "$bp_n")"
    if [ "$bp_n" -gt 0 ]; then
      local p
      for p in $bp_pids; do
        if [ -n "$KILL_CMD" ]; then $KILL_CMD "$p" 2>/dev/null || true
        else kill -TERM "$p" 2>/dev/null || true; fi
      done
    fi
  fi

  # disk: full gc (hmd's own worktrees/temp/logs/retired versions — each proof-gated).
  local receipt kb mb reclaim_mb
  receipt="$(_gc_run wet)"
  kb="$(_num "$(printf '%s' "$receipt" | _gc_kb)")"
  mb="$(_num "$(printf '%s' "$receipt" | _gc_mb)")"
  reclaim_mb=$(( (kb + 1023) / 1024 + mb ))

  if [ "$QUIET" = 1 ]; then
    # --auto: speak ONLY when something happened or a leak is being suggested.
    if [ "$reaped" -gt 0 ] || [ "$reclaim_mb" -gt 0 ]; then
      printf 'heimdall-cleanup: reaped %d orphaned hmd python (~%dMB resident), reclaimed ~%dMB disk.\n' \
        "$reaped" "$rss" "$reclaim_mb"
    elif [ "$AUTO" = 1 ] && [ "$orphans" -ge "$ORPHAN_WARN" ]; then
      printf 'heimdall-cleanup: %d orphaned hmd python detected — run `heimdall-cleanup --apply` to reap (foreign procs untouched).\n' "$orphans"
    fi
    return 0
  fi

  # The two counts stay SEPARATE in the receipt for the same reason they are separate in the
  # JSON: one number is provably heimdall's, the other is only ever "aged, detached, and not
  # yours". Merging them would let a reap of unproven processes be reported as hmd cleaning
  # up after itself.
  printf 'heimdall-cleanup --apply: reaped %d orphaned heimdall python (~%dMB resident freed; kernel-wired RAM is not heimdall'\''s to free), reclaimed ~%dMB disk.\n' \
    "$reaped" "$rss" "$reclaim_mb"
  [ "$bp_n" -gt 0 ] && printf 'heimdall-cleanup --apply: also signalled %d UNATTRIBUTED bare-stdin python orphan(s) — aged, detached, outside this session'\''s tree. Ownership was never proven; they are reported as unattributed, not as heimdall'\''s.\n' "$bp_n"
  return 0
}

# ── ADVISE (read-only system-health advisory for the SessionStart hook) ───────────
# Speaks when EITHER axis is in trouble: memory pressure is genuinely HIGH (reboot-worthy),
# OR the orphan population has crossed its floor. The two are INDEPENDENT failures — a pile
# grows whether or not RAM is tight — so gating one behind the other makes the orphan monitor
# go quiet exactly when nothing else is wrong. Whichever axis fires, the terse form is ONE
# line (the SessionStart budget), never two. Honors the opt-out. Mutates NOTHING — sysmon
# read-only, orphan scope, gc --dry-run. Never reaps.
do_advise() {
  if opted_out; then return 0; fi

  # CODE axis first, and OUTSIDE the memory gate below: dead code and RAM pressure are
  # independent failures, so a healthy machine must still hear about a subsystem nothing
  # invokes. heimdall-deadcode throttles itself (weekly by default) and prints nothing at
  # all when the audit is clean, so this costs a cache read on a normal session start.
  # This is the PERIODIC SWEEP the consumer audit rides on: SessionStart and SessionEnd
  # already run heimdall-cleanup, so there is no new schedule to invent or maintain.
  [ -x "$DEADCODE" ] && "$DEADCODE" --advise 2>/dev/null || true

  _probe_memory
  _ps_prime          # one ps snapshot shared by the orphan scope and the wired-holder probe

  # BOTH axes are measured BEFORE the gate, because either one on its own is reason enough to
  # speak. Counting orphans only after a memory check is precisely the defect this replaced:
  # on 2026-08-08 this repo carried 224 live orphans while memory read healthy, and the
  # advisory said nothing. One ps snapshot per session start is the price of not being blind.
  local pids orphans rss barepy
  pids="$(_orphan_pids)"
  orphans="$(printf '%s\n' "$pids" | grep -c . || true)"; orphans="$(_num "$orphans")"
  barepy="$(_num "$(_barepy_count)")"

  _advisory_worthy "$orphans" "$barepy" || return 0   # non-spammy: silent on a genuinely healthy machine

  # shellcheck disable=SC2086
  rss="$([ "$orphans" -gt 0 ] && _rss_mb $pids || echo 0)"

  local receipt kb mb reclaim_mb
  receipt="$(_gc_run dry)"
  kb="$(_num "$(printf '%s' "$receipt" | _gc_kb)")"
  mb="$(_num "$(printf '%s' "$receipt" | _gc_mb)")"
  reclaim_mb=$(( (kb + 1023) / 1024 + mb ))

  # ONE line by default (the SessionStart budget); the full block on --verbose. The gate
  # above is identical either way — verbosity changes what is RENDERED, never what is
  # DETECTED, so a machine under real pressure is reported in both modes.
  if [ "$VERBOSE" = 1 ]; then
    printf '[heimdall] system-health advisory:\n'
    _reboot_advisory "$orphans" "$rss" "$reclaim_mb" "$barepy"
    return 0
  fi
  # ONE line whichever axis fired. When BOTH are true the memory line takes the slot: it is
  # the more urgent reading AND it already carries both orphan counts inside it, so nothing is
  # lost by not printing a second line — and the SessionStart budget stays a hard one line.
  if _reboot_worthy; then
    _reboot_advisory_line "$orphans" "$rss" "$barepy"
  else
    _orphan_advisory_line "$orphans" "$rss" "$barepy"
  fi
  return 0
}

# ── DEEP (suggest-only handoff — reaps NOTHING) ───────────────────────────────────
do_deep() {
  if _mac_deep_clean_installed; then
    cat <<'EOF'
heimdall-cleanup --deep — heavier disk cleanup is CONFIRM-GATED, not automatic.

This step reclaims user-facing disk beyond heimdall's own footprint (SDK/simulator
bloat, dead repos, large caches). It is handed to the tiered mac-deep-clean skill,
which investigates READ-ONLY first and deletes only after you confirm each tier.

  → invoke the 'mac-deep-clean' skill (macOS) for the guided, confirm-gated sweep.

heimdall-cleanup itself never auto-deletes anything outside heimdall's own garbage.
For that safe subset run:  heimdall-cleanup --apply
EOF
  else
    cat <<'EOF'
heimdall-cleanup --deep — heavier disk cleanup is CONFIRM-GATED, not automatic.

This step would reclaim user-facing disk beyond heimdall's own footprint (SDK/simulator
bloat, dead repos, large caches) via the tiered mac-deep-clean skill — but that skill is
NOT installed on this machine, so heimdall will not tell you to invoke it.

heimdall-cleanup itself never auto-deletes anything outside heimdall's own garbage.
For that safe subset run:  heimdall-cleanup --apply

To investigate the heavier categories yourself (read-only, nothing auto-deleted):
  du -sh ~/Library/Developer/Xcode/DerivedData ~/Library/Caches/* 2>/dev/null | sort -rh | head
EOF
  fi
  return 0
}

# ── dispatch (report/advise and apply are DISJOINT — a read-only mode never reaches a reap) ──
case "$MODE" in
  report) do_report ;;
  apply)  do_apply ;;
  advise) do_advise ;;
  deep)   do_deep ;;
esac
